  "nameStory": "Frost's amanita is named after Charles Christopher Frost, a 19th-century mycologist from Vermont who dedicated his life to documenting New England's fungi. Frost was one of the first scientists to systematically study mushrooms in our region, and this colorful little amanita became his namesake species. It's fitting that a mushroom bearing his name is found throughout the very New England forests he loved to explore!",
  "description": "Frost's amanita is a small, eye-catching mushroom that shifts from bright yellow to orange-red as it matures. This petite member of the amanita family typically reaches only 2-4 inches tall with a cap that's usually smaller than a silver dollar. Unlike its larger, more imposing amanita cousins, this delicate mushroom has a slender build and vibrant coloring that makes it stand out against the forest floor. It's one of the smaller amanitas you'll encounter in New England's woodlands.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Small yellow to orange-red cap that starts bright yellow when young and develops reddish tones with age, often with white patches or scales scattered across the surface",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Slender white to pale yellow stem with a distinctive bulbous base wrapped in a white, sack-like volva that feels papery when touched",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "White gills that remain pale throughout the mushroom's life, attached close to but not touching the stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Pure white spore print when cap is placed gill-side down on dark paper overnight",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Notably small for an amanita - the entire mushroom typically fits comfortably in your palm",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Frost's amanita in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, particularly under oaks, maples, and pines. This species prefers well-drained, acidic soils and forms beneficial partnerships (mycorrhizal relationships) with tree roots. You'll often find it growing alone or in small groups on forest floors with good leaf litter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Not typically visible in spring as the mycelium is still developing underground relationships with tree roots.",
    "summer": "Prime fruiting season begins in mid to late summer, especially after warm rains when small yellow caps first emerge from the soil.",
    "fall": "Peak season continues through early fall when mature specimens show their characteristic orange-red coloring and are most easily spotted.",
    "winter": "Above-ground mushrooms disappear, but the underground fungal network remains active beneath the snow-covered forest floor."
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being one of the smallest amanitas, Frost's amanita can live for decades as an underground fungal network, only popping up its colorful mushrooms for a few weeks each year like a shy forest performer taking a brief bow!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most likely confused with the more common Fly Agaric when young, but Frost's amanita stays much smaller and develops orange-red tones rather than the classic red-with-white-spots pattern. Remember: 'Frost stays small and turns to fall colors' - it's petite like early frost and shifts to autumn hues.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Like all amanitas, this species belongs to a family that includes some of the world's most dangerous mushrooms, so the entire group requires extreme caution. The name 'amanita' comes from ancient Greek and was used by early naturalists to describe mushrooms that appeared after rain, though the exact etymology remains debated among mycologists.",
  "compareHints": "Frost's amanita is much smaller than most of its amanita relatives in New England - while species like Fly Agaric can reach 6-10 inches across, Frost's rarely exceeds 3 inches. Its yellow-to-red color progression is also distinctive, as most other regional amanitas are white, brown, or consistently red. The combination of small size, bright colors, and classic amanita features (white gills, bulbous base with volva) makes it relatively easy to distinguish from its larger cousins.",
